1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,648
Most engineers answer no: a hard
reset destroys uncommitted work.

2
00:00:08,688 --> 00:00:13,866
Two words first. Staged means recorded
with git add for the next commit, the next

3
00:00:13,906 --> 00:00:18,569
saved snapshot. And a hard reset puts
your files back to the last commit.

4
00:00:18,909 --> 00:00:23,816
The exact answer has three parts. The version
of the file that you staged: yes, it's

5
00:00:23,856 --> 00:00:26,997
an object in the repository
right now, without a name.

6
00:00:27,037 --> 00:00:30,354
An earlier version that you
staged before that: also yes.

7
00:00:30,394 --> 00:00:35,065
The line you typed after the last git add:
no, and you can prove in two commands

8
00:00:35,105 --> 00:00:38,304
that no object holds it. Hold
on to those two commands.

9
00:00:38,644 --> 00:00:43,606
Then a second question from the same
afternoon. A teammate forced a push over main,

10
00:00:43,646 --> 00:00:48,548
which replaces the server's branch instead
of extending it, and two commits by two

11
00:00:48,588 --> 00:00:52,103
people are gone from the server.
The server kept no record.

12
00:00:52,143 --> 00:00:56,201
Who did? You did, if you have fetched
before and not thrown it away.

13
00:00:58,041 --> 00:01:01,691
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

14
00:01:01,731 --> 00:01:06,408
So far in this module every recovery
concerned commits, and every commit had a

15
00:01:06,448 --> 00:01:11,307
reflog entry somewhere, a line in Git's
local log of where each name has pointed.

16
00:01:11,347 --> 00:01:14,149
Today you leave that
comfort in two directions.

17
00:01:14,489 --> 00:01:19,535
First, down to layer three: work that
became an object and has no name at all.

18
00:01:19,575 --> 00:01:24,751
A staged blob, the stored content of one
file. A stash entry, parked uncommitted

19
00:01:24,791 --> 00:01:26,251
work, that was cleared.

20
00:01:26,591 --> 00:01:31,374
Second, sideways to layer four: the remote
side. What your clone knows about the

21
00:01:31,414 --> 00:01:36,317
server, when that knowledge is wrong, and
how your clone becomes the recovery tool

22
00:01:36,357 --> 00:01:38,306
after someone else's forced push.

23
00:01:40,146 --> 00:01:43,309
state which uncommitted work has
an object and which has none;

24
00:01:43,649 --> 00:01:47,543
recover a staged file wiped by a
hard reset from a dangling blob;

25
00:01:47,883 --> 00:01:49,659
recover a cleared stash entry;

26
00:01:49,999 --> 00:01:52,105
repair broken remote-tracking state;

27
00:01:52,445 --> 00:01:56,527
restore commits overwritten by someone's
forced push from your own clone.

28
00:01:58,367 --> 00:02:03,787
Staged work. On screen, train.yaml from
today's demonstration: one edit, then git

29
00:02:03,827 --> 00:02:07,293
add. git add writes a blob
at the moment you run it.

30
00:02:07,333 --> 00:02:11,135
The index is the list of what
goes into your next commit.

31
00:02:11,475 --> 00:02:14,989
A hard reset removes the
index entries, not the blobs.

32
00:02:15,029 --> 00:02:18,741
Nothing names them, so they
are dangling, on layer three.

33
00:02:18,781 --> 00:02:24,270
A blob has no file name and no date: the
name lived in the index entry that is gone.

34
00:02:24,310 --> 00:02:29,667
You identify blobs by content. And every
git add is a checkpoint that outlives the

35
00:02:29,707 --> 00:02:33,156
index entry, so a file staged
twice leaves two blobs.

36
00:02:33,496 --> 00:02:36,621
Work typed after the last
git add was never hashed.

37
00:02:36,661 --> 00:02:40,344
No object holds it. That's the
boundary of Git's safety net.

38
00:02:40,684 --> 00:02:45,586
A dropped or cleared stash. A stash entry
is a commit with two or three parents.

39
00:02:45,626 --> 00:02:49,664
Dropping it removes its reflog
line, so the commit is unreachable.

40
00:02:49,704 --> 00:02:54,606
git stash drop prints the ID of what it
dropped. git stash clear prints nothing.

41
00:02:54,646 --> 00:02:59,795
The stash manual gives a recipe that lists
unreachable commits which look like stash

42
00:02:59,835 --> 00:03:04,490
entries. The deadline is the one of
layer three, and it's shorter than for a

43
00:03:04,530 --> 00:03:09,123
rewritten commit: a dropped stash never
has a reflog entry to fall back on.

44
00:03:09,463 --> 00:03:14,059
A force push seen from your clone. On
screen, your main and origin/main agree.

45
00:03:14,099 --> 00:03:19,052
Then you fetch after the forced push, and
origin/main moves to a commit that doesn't

46
00:03:19,092 --> 00:03:20,538
descend from the old one.

47
00:03:20,878 --> 00:03:25,792
Your remote-tracking ref has a reflog,
and it recorded the value the server had

48
00:03:25,832 --> 00:03:30,558
before. origin/main@{1} is the server's
old tip. The server itself is a bare

49
00:03:30,598 --> 00:03:34,321
repository without reflogs by
default, so it can't tell you.

50
00:03:34,361 --> 00:03:37,770
A teammate who hasn't fetched
since is another witness.

51
00:03:38,110 --> 00:03:39,785
There are two honest repairs.

52
00:03:40,125 --> 00:03:43,211
The second repair is a force
push in its own right.

53
00:03:43,251 --> 00:03:47,992
Give it an explicit expected value, the
forced commit, so that it fails if the

54
00:03:48,032 --> 00:03:49,402
server has moved again.

55
00:03:49,742 --> 00:03:54,614
Remote-tracking refs that can't be trusted.
A remote-tracking ref is a local record

56
00:03:54,654 --> 00:03:58,876
of the last contact with the server.
git status compares two local refs.

57
00:03:58,916 --> 00:04:03,492
git push talks to the server. When they
disagree, the server is right, and git

58
00:04:03,532 --> 00:04:08,404
ls-remote asks it without changing anything.
Remote-tracking refs are derived data:

59
00:04:08,444 --> 00:04:11,600
remove what is broken and
let a fetch write the truth.

60
00:04:11,940 --> 00:04:16,720
When not to do any of this: the reflex
"get in sync" with git reset --hard

61
00:04:16,760 --> 00:04:21,995
origin/main after a forced update. It
removes the lost commits from the one local

62
00:04:22,035 --> 00:04:23,819
branch that still held them.

63
00:04:25,659 --> 00:04:30,890
Now a picture to keep. In the vault, a
staged file is a box that was put on a shelf

64
00:04:30,930 --> 00:04:34,192
with a sticky label on the
counter, the index entry.

65
00:04:34,232 --> 00:04:39,463
The reset swept the counter. The box is on
the shelf with no label on it at all: no

66
00:04:39,503 --> 00:04:44,290
file name, no date. You find it by
opening boxes and reading what is inside.

67
00:04:44,630 --> 00:04:48,093
A line you typed and never
staged was never put in a box.

68
00:04:48,133 --> 00:04:50,245
No audit of the vault will find it.

69
00:04:50,585 --> 00:04:55,423
For the remote side, picture two branch
offices. Your office keeps a photocopy of

70
00:04:55,463 --> 00:05:00,423
the other office's index cards, taken at
your last visit, and a ledger of how those

71
00:05:00,463 --> 00:05:05,302
photocopies changed. When the other office
rewrites a card and keeps no ledger of

72
00:05:05,342 --> 00:05:10,241
its own, your photocopy ledger is the only
written record of what the card used to

73
00:05:10,281 --> 00:05:13,674
say. The model breaks if you
treat the photocopy as live.

74
00:05:13,714 --> 00:05:16,083
It's only as current as your last fetch.

75
00:05:17,923 --> 00:05:23,689
Six kinds of work. For each one ask the
first of the three questions: was it ever an

76
00:05:23,729 --> 00:05:27,559
object? Try it now, thirty
seconds. Answer for each one.

77
00:05:27,599 --> 00:05:28,665
Say it out loud.

78
00:05:33,505 --> 00:05:37,004
The first three say yes. The
fourth and fifth say no.

79
00:05:37,344 --> 00:05:42,037
Here is the full table, with what still
names each kind of work after the accident.

80
00:05:42,077 --> 00:05:45,287
For the fourth and fifth rows
you stop using Git at once.

81
00:05:47,127 --> 00:05:52,607
sweep.yaml is new and staged. train.yaml
has a staged change and a later edit that

82
00:05:52,647 --> 00:05:56,108
isn't staged: that is the
MM. notes.md is untracked.

83
00:05:56,448 --> 00:06:00,194
Predict the fate of each of the
three files. Say it out loud.

84
00:06:00,234 --> 00:06:00,815
I'll wait.

85
00:06:05,655 --> 00:06:10,517
notes.md survived, because a reset
doesn't touch untracked files.

86
00:06:10,557 --> 00:06:15,797
sweep.yaml is deleted and train.yaml
is back at the committed version.

87
00:06:15,837 --> 00:06:21,981
Stop. Classify: staged, so layer three. 🟢
SAFE: git fsck --lost-found writes files

88
00:06:22,021 --> 00:06:25,526
under .git/lost-found and
changes nothing else.

89
00:06:25,866 --> 00:06:29,284
Predict the number of dangling
blobs. Two files were staged.

90
00:06:29,624 --> 00:06:35,196
Three blobs for two files, because
sweep.yaml was staged twice and each git add

91
00:06:35,236 --> 00:06:37,966
wrote a blob. Identify them by content.

92
00:06:43,306 --> 00:06:49,669
Both staged versions are back. The third
blob, 0697c1f, is the earlier staging of

93
00:06:49,709 --> 00:06:50,539
sweep.yaml.

94
00:06:50,879 --> 00:06:55,551
Now what isn't there. The line early_stopping:
true was typed after the last git

95
00:06:55,591 --> 00:06:55,787
add.

96
00:06:56,127 --> 00:07:00,032
git hash-object computes the ID
that the full file would have had.

97
00:07:00,072 --> 00:07:01,825
No object with that ID exists.

98
00:07:02,165 --> 00:07:04,936
The boundary of the safety
net, drawn in two commands.

99
00:07:04,976 --> 00:07:07,227
Those are the two commands
from the opening.

100
00:07:07,567 --> 00:07:10,057
The stash, in a second
copy of the project.

101
00:07:10,397 --> 00:07:14,648
No output, no IDs. Here's the
recipe from the stash manual.

102
00:07:14,688 --> 00:07:20,684
There were two entries. Quick quiz, three
options: does it find none, one, or both?

103
00:07:20,724 --> 00:07:21,848
Say it out loud.

104
00:07:26,688 --> 00:07:32,768
One. The recipe ends in --grep=WIP. The
default message of a stash is "WIP on" the

105
00:07:32,808 --> 00:07:38,589
branch. An entry made with git stash push
-m is named "On" the branch and your

106
00:07:38,629 --> 00:07:44,858
message, and doesn't match. Leave the --grep
out and rely on --merges: a stash entry

107
00:07:44,898 --> 00:07:49,112
is a merge commit, and few
other unreachable commits are.

108
00:07:49,452 --> 00:07:54,400
git stash store is 🟢 SAFE: it sets
refs/stash and adds one reflog line.

109
00:07:54,440 --> 00:07:56,718
The entries are back in the list.

110
00:07:57,058 --> 00:08:01,660
The plus sign and "(forced update)" mean
that origin/main didn't move forward: the

111
00:08:01,700 --> 00:08:06,133
server's branch was replaced by a commit
that doesn't descend from the old one.

112
00:08:06,473 --> 00:08:11,948
Status calls it divergence: ahead 2, behind
1. On the picture, two commits only you

113
00:08:11,988 --> 00:08:15,603
have, and one only the server
has. Don't "get in sync".

114
00:08:15,643 --> 00:08:16,799
Read the evidence.

115
00:08:17,139 --> 00:08:22,659
origin/main@{1} is the server's old tip,
d8a3934. The two ranges are the damage

116
00:08:22,699 --> 00:08:28,290
report: two commits by two authors were
removed from the server, and one commit,

117
00:08:28,330 --> 00:08:30,542
882b940, was put in their place.

118
00:08:30,882 --> 00:08:35,733
The server kept no record. Here the forced
commit is legitimate work, so the repair

119
00:08:35,773 --> 00:08:36,616
is "keep both".

120
00:08:41,756 --> 00:08:47,643
The push is a fast-forward, 882b940..d967a92:
the server's tip is an ancestor of the

121
00:08:47,683 --> 00:08:52,159
merge commit, the commit with two
parents that joins both lines.

122
00:08:52,199 --> 00:08:58,015
Every clone that already has the old commits,
and every CI record that quotes their

123
00:08:58,055 --> 00:09:03,871
IDs, stays valid. Rebasing the lost commits
onto the forced one would give them new

124
00:09:03,911 --> 00:09:05,706
IDs and create duplicates.

125
00:09:06,046 --> 00:09:10,016
Two local refs agree, and the
server disagrees. Ask the server.

126
00:09:10,356 --> 00:09:15,587
Two discrepancies. origin/main holds an ID
that the server doesn't have at all, and

127
00:09:15,627 --> 00:09:19,207
origin/feature/hybrid-search
is reported as a broken ref.

128
00:09:19,247 --> 00:09:20,859
The ordinary repair fails.

129
00:09:21,199 --> 00:09:25,851
badRefContent names the damaged file. The
dangling commit is worth reading: the

130
00:09:25,891 --> 00:09:30,781
fetch downloaded the server's new commit
and then failed before it could update any

131
00:09:30,821 --> 00:09:31,018
ref.

132
00:09:31,358 --> 00:09:36,380
The ref file is empty. And the newest
reflog entry of origin/main has no message.

133
00:09:36,420 --> 00:09:39,879
A fetch writes "fetch:", a
push writes "update by push".

134
00:09:39,919 --> 00:09:45,003
An entry without a message was written by
git update-ref from a script or by hand.

135
00:09:45,343 --> 00:09:49,977
The fetch corrected origin/main with a
forced update and recreated the other ref.

136
00:09:50,017 --> 00:09:53,786
Status now tells the truth: two
commits to push, one to integrate.

137
00:09:55,626 --> 00:09:57,274
Five mistakes to watch for.

138
00:09:57,614 --> 00:10:02,810
"A hard reset destroyed it, nothing to
do." Root cause: git add wrote a blob for

139
00:10:02,850 --> 00:10:07,195
every staged version, and the reset
removed only the index entries.

140
00:10:07,535 --> 00:10:12,741
Searching for a staged file by name. Root
cause: the file name lived in the index

141
00:10:12,781 --> 00:10:14,748
entry; a blob has content only.

142
00:10:15,088 --> 00:10:20,402
Running the stash recipe with --grep=WIP
and concluding that an entry is gone.

143
00:10:20,442 --> 00:10:25,070
Root cause: entries made with a
message are named "On <branch>: ..."

144
00:10:25,110 --> 00:10:26,786
and do not contain "WIP".

145
00:10:27,126 --> 00:10:30,642
git reset --hard origin/main
after a forced update.

146
00:10:30,682 --> 00:10:36,290
Root cause: it removes the lost commits
from the one local branch that still held

147
00:10:36,330 --> 00:10:39,148
them; they remain only in reflog entries.

148
00:10:39,488 --> 00:10:44,434
Trusting git status about the server. Root
cause: status compares local refs, and a

149
00:10:44,474 --> 00:10:49,419
remote-tracking ref is a record of the last
contact, which can be stale or damaged.

150
00:10:51,259 --> 00:10:55,830
Now, out of the lab. A data platform
team shares main of a question-answering

151
00:10:55,870 --> 00:11:00,859
service. On Monday morning one engineer's
first fetch prints a plus sign and "forced

152
00:11:00,899 --> 00:11:05,769
update". Two commits from Friday, an answer
cache by one colleague and its metrics

153
00:11:05,809 --> 00:11:10,678
by another, are no longer on the server.
In their place is one commit that renames

154
00:11:10,718 --> 00:11:11,517
settings keys.

155
00:11:11,857 --> 00:11:16,816
She doesn't pull and doesn't reset. She
reads the reflog of origin/main and takes

156
00:11:16,856 --> 00:11:21,815
the entry before the forced update. Two
range listings give her the damage report

157
00:11:21,855 --> 00:11:25,024
with authors, and she posts
it with full commit IDs.

158
00:11:25,064 --> 00:11:30,147
The team decides that the rename is legitimate
work, so she merges origin/main into

159
00:11:30,187 --> 00:11:33,664
her main, which still has the
Friday commits, and pushes.

160
00:11:34,004 --> 00:11:38,785
No force is needed. The colleague who
forced the push learns what a lease is, and

161
00:11:38,825 --> 00:11:42,117
the repository gets a rule
against force pushes to main.

162
00:11:42,157 --> 00:11:45,390
That's GitHub's side, and it
comes later in the course.

163
00:11:47,230 --> 00:11:53,019
Your turn. Do Lab 12.8, "Overwritten
changes and a cleared stash", in

164
00:11:53,059 --> 00:11:55,299
lab-manual/m12-recovery.md.

165
00:11:55,639 --> 00:12:01,458
Before the reset, write down for each file
whether an object exists for its current

166
00:12:01,498 --> 00:12:05,693
content, and how many blobs git
fsck will report afterwards.

167
00:12:05,733 --> 00:12:11,551
Before you run the stash recipe, predict
how many entries it lists with and without

168
00:12:11,591 --> 00:12:12,327
--grep=WIP.

169
00:12:12,667 --> 00:12:18,312
The challenge is Lab 12.11, "A force push,
seen from the local side", in the same

170
00:12:18,352 --> 00:12:18,662
file.

171
00:12:20,502 --> 00:12:21,769
Pause and answer out loud.

172
00:12:26,609 --> 00:12:31,297
A strong answer divides the file's history
into what was staged and what was typed

173
00:12:31,337 --> 00:12:35,678
afterwards, and gives the reason for
each half in terms of when an object is

174
00:12:35,718 --> 00:12:40,348
written. It names the tool, says what form
the findings take and how you tell one

175
00:12:40,388 --> 00:12:45,133
from another, since they carry no names.
It states the deadline and what starts it.

176
00:12:45,173 --> 00:12:49,918
And it doesn't stop at "the rest is gone":
it shows how you would demonstrate that,

177
00:12:49,958 --> 00:12:52,340
and where outside Git you would look next.

178
00:12:54,180 --> 00:12:57,107
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

179
00:12:57,447 --> 00:13:03,080
Every git add writes a blob; after a hard
reset those blobs are dangling and git

180
00:13:03,120 --> 00:13:06,129
fsck --lost-found writes
them out as files.

181
00:13:06,469 --> 00:13:10,720
Content that was never staged was never
an object, and I can show that with git

182
00:13:10,760 --> 00:13:12,295
hash-object and git cat-file.

183
00:13:12,635 --> 00:13:17,584
A cleared stash entry is an unreachable
merge commit; git stash store puts it back.

184
00:13:17,924 --> 00:13:23,507
After someone's forced push, origin/main@{1}
in my clone is the server's old tip.

185
00:13:23,847 --> 00:13:27,658
When status and push disagree, I
ask the server with git ls-remote.

186
00:13:29,498 --> 00:13:36,568
Read sections 13.9 and 13.10 of Chapter
13. Do Lab 12.10, "Broken remote-tracking

187
00:13:36,608 --> 00:13:41,748
state, and a corrupt index",
in lab-manual/m12-recovery.md.

188
00:13:41,788 --> 00:13:49,034
Do Exercise 12.5, Level 2, "One file from
a commit that only the reflog knows", and

189
00:13:49,074 --> 00:13:55,179
Exercise 12.7, Level 3, "A rejected
push after "a small addition"", in

190
00:13:55,219 --> 00:13:59,042
exercises/m11-m15-investigation-recovery.md.

191
00:13:59,382 --> 00:14:04,497
Today you recovered work that had no name
at all, and you used your own clone as the

192
00:14:04,537 --> 00:14:09,530
witness after a forced push. Do the
overwritten changes lab before the next video.

193
00:14:09,570 --> 00:14:13,274
Next time: a damaged repository,
and what can't be recovered.

194
00:14:13,314 --> 00:14:16,466
Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

195
00:14:16,506 --> 00:14:17,939
See you in the next one.
